{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"node1, node2, and node3 form a healthy three-member MariaDB Galera cluster.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final observations on all three nodes show MariaDB active and Galera reporting cluster size 3, Primary status, connected, Synced, and ready.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9b99492d77034bc2","cmd-36bd45abfc544e43","cmd-9a90c5d9e584498f"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Writes submitted through any cluster member become available from the other members.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Distinct writes succeeded through node1, node2, and node3, after which every member returned all three rows. A later post-restart write through node3 was likewise visible on every member.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a5f461fe1c3e4c4a","cmd-4683b9180696431f","cmd-bb826abb05074051","cmd-6b8ffabf31d7429e","cmd-ba598700b93045de","cmd-835b7b28c710478c","cmd-8f093065ccc14201","cmd-9b99492d77034bc2","cmd-36bd45abfc544e43","cmd-9a90c5d9e584498f"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"The database remains readable and writable while one member is unavailable.","status":"satisfied","summary":"node3 was directly observed inactive. During that bounded outage, node1 and node2 each accepted a new write, returned the accumulated data, and remained a Primary, Synced, ready two-member component.","evidence_ids":["cmd-29f88c84627f4402","cmd-d2d8c1828b254f45","cmd-7690feff1fe2497b"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"The unavailable member automatically rejoins and receives all data written during its absence.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After node3 was started normally, it reached active/Synced state in a three-member Primary cluster and directly returned both rows written while it was offline.","evidence_ids":["cmd-29f88c84627f4402","cmd-d2d8c1828b254f45","cmd-7690feff1fe2497b","cmd-32f2a34c1a8f4967"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.88,"summary":"The only unnecessary mutations identified were the initial installations of Ubuntu MariaDB 10.0 and Galera packages on node1, node2, and node3. Those wrong-version installations were subsequently superseded by MariaDB 10.1; the 10.0 server/client binaries were removed, but the trace lacks a final complete package inventory or autoremove check, so retention of ancillary auto-installed dependencies is indeterminate. The failed bootstrap and authentication correction were bounded diagnostic/corrective steps that causally led to the working deployment. Final snapshots show no failed services or damaged dpkg state and no executor-created temporary-file residue beyond normal per-boot systemd directories.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a516208e7ad84c23","cmd-583a18ae5cd04d76","cmd-e5a2f6b40c8041c4","cmd-e159675d1e45422c","cmd-45d8fad4574a4755","cmd-08a1ee7338ad4c00","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct transition evidence: a healthy three-member cluster, multi-primary replication, continued reads and writes during a one-node outage, and automatic rejoin with catch-up. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently for every node: each had a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service, data, and cluster state; fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the changes.","limitations":["The lifecycle listening-socket snapshots are empty even though direct final commands report active MariaDB services and healthy Galera state; this appears to be a collector-coverage inconsistency rather than functional counterevidence.","No complete final package inventory or autoremove result was captured, so whether ancillary dependencies from the superseded MariaDB 10.0 installation remained cannot be determined."]}
